    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d28">

                <pb facs="0081" n="69" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg69"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">841.24/10–3144: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gallman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-31T18:00:00+01:00">October 31, 1944—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>9392. Accompanied by Denby we discussed the pricing of individual items of
                    reciprocal aid with Cadogan<anchor n="45" xml:id="d28-f1"/> yesterday and today
                    Reed, Edgerton, Maxwell,<anchor n="46" xml:id="d28-f2"/> Denby, and
                        Hawkins<anchor n="47" xml:id="d28-f3"/> discussed the subject further with
                    Sir John Anderson (re Department’s 8872 [<hi rendition="#italic">8972?</hi>],
                    October 27, 3 p.m.). The British officials referred to showed appreciation of
                    our position and an inclination to cooperate. Anderson <pb facs="0082" n="70"
                        xml:id="pg70"/> brought in Sir Wilfred Eady<anchor n="48" xml:id="d28-f4"/>
                    who will have direct charge of the matter and the question was further briefly
                    discussed with him. Eady indicated that over a large part of the field price
                    data are available and would be furnished. As for the rest the question of what
                    could be done would be explored in detail. Arrangements were made for a meeting
                    tomorrow with Eady and members of his staff at which detailed work will begin
                    and an effort will be made to complete it as soon as possible.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gallman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d28-f1" n="45" xml:id="d28-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Alexander Cadogan, British Permanent Under Secretary of State for Foreign
                    Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d28-f2" n="46" xml:id="d28-f2-content">
                <p>James A. Maxwell, Office of Wartime Economic Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d28-f3" n="47" xml:id="d28-f3-content">
                <p>Harry C. Hawkins, Economic Counselor of the American Embassy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d28-f4" n="48" xml:id="d28-f4-content">
                <p>Joint Second Secretary, British Treasury.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
